    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Could you go a bit deeper into the specific failure modes? I'm particularly interested in what happens when the thermal management fails—like when the system starts acting up due to uneven heat distribution or sudden temperature spikes. What are the physical indicators of that instability?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When thermal management fails, the system can experience instability and uneven heat distribution, leading to several physical indicators. In semi-metallic pads, uneven heat distribution can cause a phenomenon called \"pad creep,\" where the friction material begins to deform and lose its shape, affecting braking performance. Sudden temperature spikes can also lead to \"pad chipping\" or \" pad melting,\" where the surface layer of the friction material is eroded or deformed, compromising brake effectiveness. Additionally, overheating can cause the brake rotor to become deformed or warped, leading to vibration and noise. In carbon-ceramic discs, thermal instability can result in \"amaturation\" - the formation of cracks or micro-oxidation, which can thicken the disc material and reduce its thermal conductivity.",
      "source": "target",
